<root BTCPP_format="4">
    <!--
        S2 (CALIBRATION-FREE): descend until the down-cam sees the table, then
        center over it with vision. Needs NO pool-specific dead-reckon constants
        (unlike OctagonTableMission) and does NO pinger homing / NO grasp, so it is
        reusable by both the pinger-homed OctagonRestoreMission and the
        OctagonMission capstone. It works whether the sub arrived via S1 acoustic
        homing or was hand-placed above the octagon.

        Two things make the SearchForTarget spiral genuinely reachable, and BOTH
        are required; it was dead code without either:

        1. min_area_frac="0.15". The model emits a confident corner phantom
           'table' in ~every frame, so without an area floor all three presence
           checks read "table in frame" forever and nothing ever searches.
           See detection_gate.hpp.
        2. ForceSuccess around DescendUntilDetected. This is a Sequence, so a
           descend FAILURE used to abort the whole subtree before the Fallback
           below could tick. The original comment here reasoned only about
           DetectTarget failing AFTER a successful descent, but at a start
           offset laterally from the table, descending is exactly what fails,
           and that is precisely when the spiral is needed. Descending 2.4 m
           cannot correct a 1.5 m LATERAL offset.

        Measured 2026-07-28 (run_task 5, stage `approach`, `near` start): with (1)
        alone the descent correctly rejected all 159 phantom boxes over 452
        frames, then failed the subtree at 45 s and never spiralled.

        min_z on the descend is the third piece: see below.
    -->
    <BehaviorTree ID="AcquireTable">
        <Sequence>
            <!-- min_conf 0.20 is a temporary value to get Task 5 working in sim
                 while the YOLO model is being retrained. Once the model is good,
                 make confidence 0.80 or higher, most likely. min_area_frac does
                 the real discrimination meanwhile. -->
            <!-- Descend step-by-step until the table appears on the down cam.
                 ForceSuccess: failing to find the table by descending is not a
                 mission failure, it is the case the spiral below exists to
                 handle. Without this the Sequence aborts here and the spiral
                 never ticks.

                 min_z="-0.55" is a hard depth floor. max_steps is a STEP COUNT,
                 not a depth bound (12 x 0.20 m = 2.4 m, which from z=-0.35
                 reaches -2.75), and the tabletop is at -0.85 with props resting
                 on it at -0.80. A measured run sank to -1.41, 0.56 m BELOW the
                 tabletop, where a down camera cannot see the table at any
                 lateral offset, so both the descent AND the following spiral
                 were doomed. -0.55 keeps 0.30 m of clearance over the tabletop
                 and ~0.15 m over the tallest prop, and the table already fills
                 0.80-0.94 of the frame at -0.35, so little descent is needed to
                 clear the 0.15 area floor anyway. -->
            <ForceSuccess>
                <DescendUntilDetected label="table" camera="down" min_conf="0.20" min_area_frac="0.15"
                                      step_m="0.20" max_steps="12" min_z="-0.55"
                                      timeout_msec="45000" ctx="{ctx}"/>
            </ForceSuccess>
            <!-- Make sure the table is in frame; spiral-search if it drifted off. -->
            <Fallback>
                <DetectTarget label="table" camera="down" min_conf="0.20" min_area_frac="0.15" ctx="{ctx}"/>
                <SearchForTarget label="table" camera="down" min_conf="0.20" min_area_frac="0.15"
                                 step_m="0.30" max_radius_m="2.0"
                                 timeout_msec="60000" ctx="{ctx}"/>
            </Fallback>
            <!-- Center over the table with the world-frame lock (LockOverTarget)
                 rather than the incremental pixel hone: the table lies exactly on
                 the cast plane (table_z), so the absolute-goal, level-holding lock
                 removes the pitch-latch/moving-setpoint divergence that made the
                 old CenterCamera hone drift off and starve SelectTarget. Degrades
                 gracefully if unseen. table_z is the tabletop world z. -->
            <SubTree ID="LockOverTarget" _autoremap="true"
                     label="table" camera="down" select="largest"
                     min_conf="0.20" min_area_frac="0.15"
                     table_z="-0.85" tol_world="0.05" timeout_msec="20000" miss_frames="5" ctx="{ctx}"/>
        </Sequence>
    </BehaviorTree>
</root>
